# AirBnB Clone - The Console

## Description

This project is the first step towards building a full-stack clone of the
AirBnB web application. It lays the foundation for everything that comes
after it: a command interpreter that can create, read, update, and destroy
the core objects of the application (`User`, `State`, `City`, `Amenity`,
`Place`, `Review`), and a storage engine that saves those objects to a JSON
file so they persist between sessions.

Later stages of the AirBnB clone project will build on top of this console:
HTML/CSS templating, database storage, a REST API, and a dynamic front end
will all reuse the classes and storage engine created here.

## Command Interpreter

The command interpreter is a custom shell, built with Python's `cmd`
module, for managing the objects of the AirBnB clone project. It behaves
like a limited version of a Unix shell, scoped to one job: creating,
retrieving, updating, and destroying the application's objects.

### How to start it

Clone the repository, then from the root of the project run:

```
$ ./console.py
```

Alternatively:

```
$ python3 console.py
```

This drops you into an interactive prompt:

```
(hbnb)
```

The console also works in non-interactive mode, e.g. piping commands into
it:

```
$ echo "help" | ./console.py
```

### How to use it

Once inside the console, you can manage objects with the following
commands:

| Command   | Description                                              |
|-----------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| `help`    | Shows available commands or help on a specific command    |
| `create`  | Creates a new instance of a class, saves it, prints its id|
| `show`    | Prints the string representation of an instance           |
| `destroy` | Deletes an instance based on class name and id             |
| `all`     | Prints string representations of all instances            |
| `update`  | Updates an instance's attribute                            |
| `quit`    | Exits the console                                          |
| `EOF`     | Exits the console (Ctrl+D)                                  |

Supported classes: `BaseModel`, `User`, `State`, `City`, `Amenity`,
`Place`, `Review`.

### Examples

Starting the console and viewing help:

```
$ ./console.py
(hbnb) help

Documented commands (type help <topic>):
========================================
EOF  help  quit

(hbnb) quit
$
```

Creating and inspecting an object:

```
(hbnb) create BaseModel
49faff9a-6318-451f-87b6-910505c55907
(hbnb) show BaseModel 49faff9a-6318-451f-87b6-910505c55907
[BaseModel] (49faff9a-6318-451f-87b6-910505c55907) {'id': '49faff9a-6318-451f-87b6-910505c55907', ...}
(hbnb) all BaseModel
["[BaseModel] (49faff9a-6318-451f-87b6-910505c55907) {...}"]
(hbnb) destroy BaseModel 49faff9a-6318-451f-87b6-910505c55907
(hbnb) quit
```

*(This section will be updated with real output once the console commands
are implemented.)*

## Environment

- All files are interpreted on Ubuntu 20.04 LTS using Python 3.8.5
- Code follows the `pycodestyle` (2.7.x) style guide
- Every module, class, and function is documented

